## 2026-06-12 (T-GEN ANALYTIC lane, branch `feat/tgen-analytic-step`)

The "(AAW) smooth ⇝ analytic" factor — the deepest piece of the
continuous ⇝ smooth ⇝ analytic reduction underlying T-GEN — landed as
`Jacobians/RiemannSurface/SmoothAnalyticLoop.lean` (all headlines
kernel-verified **standard-3 only**, no sorryAx, no project axioms):

- **Key finding (cheapest-sufficient formulation).** The predicate an
  `AnalyticLoop` must satisfy is the moving-chart, pointwise condition
  `hmov : ∀ r, AnalyticAt ℝ (fun u => extChartAt 𝓘(ℂ) (γ r) (γ u)) r`. This is
  *literally* "the chart readout is `C^ω` at every parameter": packaged as
  `IsAnalyticCurve` and proven equal to `hmov` (`isAnalyticCurve_iff_movingChart`,
  via `ContDiffAt.analyticAt` / `AnalyticAt.contDiffAt`). So a genuinely
  real-analytic loop needs **no approximation at all**:
  `AnalyticLoop.ofAnalyticCurve` packages any continuous, closed,
  `IsAnalyticCurve` loop as an `AnalyticLoop` **unconditionally**.

- **Honest Grauert gap.** Turning a merely *smooth* (`C^∞`) loop into an
  analytic one rel endpoints is genuinely Grauert/Whitney and Mathlib-absent.
  Gemini chat confirmed the obstruction is global: a loop leaves any single
  chart, and gluing `C^ω` chart-approximations fails (no non-constant analytic
  partition of unity); the standard proof needs a real-analytic embedding
  `X ↪ ℝ^N` + analytic tubular retraction, neither in Mathlib. Even *stating*
  `ContMDiff … ω` for a curve into `X` is blocked: the `IsManifold 𝓘(ℝ, ℂ) ω X`
  instance (complex-analytic ⇒ real-analytic manifold) is absent
  (`inferInstance` fails), which is *why* `IsAnalyticCurve` is phrased via the
  chart readout rather than `ContMDiff`.

- **Named residual + conditional target.** `SmoothLoopAnalyticApprox x₀` is the
  single named Grauert hypothesis (a `Prop`, not a global `axiom` — consumers
  see it explicitly); `analyticLoop_homotopic_of_smooth` proves the target
  (smooth loop ⇒ homotopic-rel-endpoints `AnalyticLoop`) conditional on it.
  `Path.Homotopic` (auto rel-endpoints for paths) is the homotopy relation;
  `curveToPath` packages a raw curve as the `Path x₀ x₀` to compare against
  `loopToPath δ`.

- **Go/no-go on closing smooth ⇝ analytic this session: NO-GO via Fourier.**
  The periodic/Fourier route does *not* avoid Grauert on a general X — it only
  works inside a single chart, and the global gluing is the real wall. The
  win is structural: the moving-chart definition makes the *analytic* case
  free, isolating the residual to exactly one classical theorem.
